  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nToday will feature very warm and mainly sunny weather as southwest \nwinds increase between 6-15 mph. This will advect in some very warm \nair into the region, with 925mb temperatures approaching +16C over \nthe Finger Lakes and 850mb temperatures around +10C. This pattern \nsupports near record highs ranging from the mid-60s to mid-70s over \nthe region.\n\nA weak, moisture starved cold front approaches from the north this \nevening, dropping through the area overnight. This feature will \nbring a narrow band of decaying showers along it, with less than a \ntenth of an inch of rain over Central NY...fading to no\nmeasurable rain expected over most of NE PA. Winds begin to \nshift northwesterly late tonight, but overnight lows remain \nabove average in the mid-40s to lower 50s. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nFriday features gusty west-northwest winds up to 25 mph and cooler \ntemperatures in the 50s to low 60s. There will be some lingering \nclouds across Central NY through the day, with more sunshine for\nNE PA.\n\nHigh pressure builds over the area Friday night, Saturday and into \nSunday. This will bring seasonable and dry weather with some \nincreasing clouds possible by later Sunday.\n\nWeak rounds of overrunning mid level moisture stream into the region \nMonday and Tuesday night week. This will bring a bit more cloud \ncover at times, but upper level ridging continues to redevelop over \nthe area during this time. This upper level subsidence, combined \nwith stubborn low level dry air will make measurable rainfall \nunlikely during this time frame.\n\nA slow moving upper level low looks to spin across the Midwest, \nslowly heading east toward the middle and end of the upcoming work \nweek. There remains uncertainty in the timing of this frontal system \nas it moves east. Eventually shower chances do increase in the \nensemble data, especially by next Thursday. We have plenty of time \nto watch this system, but this could bring our next best chance for \nmeasurable rain to portions of the forecast area.",
